bevy-horizon: keep a one-warp workgroup's barriers, for emulators (NAK_DEBUG=wgbar)

For mesa-switch (https://github.com/danfromtico/mesa-switch, by danfromtico and NaGaa95; MIT)
at 606441c9, after 0001-0021. scripts/build_nvk.sh applies it; this text before the first
"diff --git" line is ignored by git apply.

When a compute shader's workgroup is one warp (32 threads or fewer), NAK turns its workgroup
barriers into subgroup ones and then drops their execution part, because it keeps the warp
converged and a warp's threads run together. So no BAR.SYNC is emitted, only the memory barrier
if there is one. On the GPU that is right.

Ryujinx translates the Maxwell shader for the host, where a guest warp becomes a workgroup of
32 host invocations. On llvmpipe (lavapipe), whose subgroups are 8 wide, those run as four
subgroups of 8 that wait for each other only at a host barrier. With none, the first 8 threads
run the shader to its end before the next 8 start, and whatever the threads hand each other
through workgroup memory comes out wrong.

With NAK_DEBUG=wgbar, NAK keeps such a barrier's execution scope, so it emits BAR.SYNC as it
does for a workgroup of more than one warp, and Ryujinx emits a host barrier for it. It is off
by default, in Switch builds too, and is only for running in Ryujinx.

